layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | The
    strong Inbox
    | is where Claude's requests wait for you while
    a(href="/docs/walk-mode") walk mode
    | is on. Each request is a card with everything you need to decide, and the buttons to answer it. It is designed to
    | be used with one thumb on a phone. The number on the Inbox tab is how many cards are waiting.
  include "partials/shot.faqir" (assets=site.assets file="inbox-phone.png" alt="The Inbox on a phone: the Walk mode and Pause everything switches, then a Plan card with the plan text and an Approve plan button." caption="The Inbox on a phone, with walk mode on and a plan waiting for approval." phone)

  h2#switches The two switches at the top
  dl
    dt
      strong Walk mode
    dd
      | The global walk mode switch. On: "permissions, questions and plans wait for you here". Off: "hooks only watch;
      | nothing waits for you". The card gets an amber border while it is on.
    dt
      strong Pause everything
    dd
      | Holds every session at its next tool call until you switch it off again. Useful when something is going wrong on
      | several machines at once. Paused sessions do not create cards and do not notify you.
  p Both switch instantly and switch back if the server could not save the change.

  h2#cards Reading a card
  p Every card starts with the same information:
  ul
    li
      | A
      strong kind
      | badge:
      span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="warning") Permission
      | ,
      span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="primary") Question
      | ,
      span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="secondary") Plan
      | or
      span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="success") Turn finished
      | , the tool involved and how long ago it arrived.
    li The session's name (tap it to open the session), the machine and project, the folder and the branch.
    li
      strong Claude said
      | : Claude's last message before the request, so you know the context. Long messages are clipped; tap
      strong Show more
      | .
  p
    | Opening a notification takes you to the Inbox with that card highlighted.

  h2#permission Permission cards
  p
    | Claude wants to use a tool that needs your approval, such as running a command or editing a file. The card shows
    | exactly what it wants to do: the full command for shell commands, a red and green diff for edits, the file and its
    | size for new files, the address for web requests.
  dl
    dt
      strong Allow once
    dd Lets this one call run.
    dt
      strong Allow for this session
    dd
      | Only shown when Claude Code offered a matching rule (for example "always allow npm run build"). It allows this call
      | and every similar one for the rest of this Claude session.
    dt
      strong Deny
    dd
      | Refuses the call. Claude is told why and carries on with something else. Write the reason in
      strong Tell Claude why (optional)
      | , for example "use the staging script instead"; otherwise it hears "Denied from Buildyboy".
    dt
      strong Deny and interrupt
    dd Refuses the call and also stops Claude's current turn, so it waits for new instructions.
  +callout(variant="info")
    @content
      p
        | Permission notifications on most phones and desktops have
        strong Allow
        | and
        strong Deny
        | buttons, so you can answer without opening the app. Those buttons work for 15 minutes.

  h2#question Question cards
  p
    | Claude asked you to choose (its "AskUserQuestion" tool). Each question shows its options as large tappable rows
    | with a short description; questions marked "(choose any)" accept several answers. Tap
    strong Other…
    | to type your own answer instead. When every question has an answer, press
    strong Send answer
    | .

  h2#plan Plan cards
  p
    | Claude is in plan mode and proposes a plan before changing anything. Read it, then either press
    strong Approve plan
    | to let it start, or type what should change in
    strong What should change?
    | and press
    strong Reject with feedback
    | . Claude revises the plan and asks again.

  h2#turn-finished Turn finished cards
  p
    | With walk mode on, every time Claude finishes a turn it waits a few minutes (5 by default) for your next
    | instruction instead of going idle straight away. This card is that moment. You can:
  ul
    li
      | tap a quick reply:
      strong continue
      | ,
      strong run the tests
      | ,
      strong commit and push
      | or
      strong stop here
      | (each is sent at once);
    li
      | type an instruction in
      strong Or tell Claude what to do next
      | and press
      strong Send reply
      | ;
    li
      | or press
      strong Let it go idle
      | to release it now.
  p
    | Whatever you send becomes Claude's next instruction and the session keeps working. If you do nothing, it goes idle
    | when the wait is over, exactly as it would have without Buildyboy.

  h2#history History
  p
    | Under the cards,
    strong History
    | lists the last 50 requests and what happened to them. Each row has a badge:
  ul
    li
      span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="success") answered
      | : someone answered it. The summary says how ("Allowed once", "Denied: use the staging script", "Answered: Postgres",
      | "Replied: run the tests") and from where (by web, by push, by local…).
    li
      span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="warning") timeout
      | : nobody answered in time, so Claude Code's normal behaviour took over ("Timed out — the local prompt applied").
      | The same wording is used when a server restart cleared the request.
    li
      span(data-ui="badge") superseded
      | : it was answered at the terminal before you got to it ("Answered at the terminal").

  h2#errors When answering fails
  ul
    li
      strong "Already answered (probably at your desk)."
      | The request is no longer waiting. Usually you answered it at the terminal, but it is also what you see when it
      | timed out or the session was stopped.
    li
      strong "Could not send: …"
      | The server could not be reached or refused the answer. Check your connection and try again; the card stays until
      | it is answered or times out.
  p
    | The Inbox refreshes itself live. Cards disappear as soon as they are answered anywhere, including from another
    | device or from the terminal.
